            <ArticleTitle>Age-related differences in cardiovascular reactions to mental stress tests in women.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>18-34</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Tested 84 healthy, sedentary women in the laboratory during performance of difficult and easy problem-solving tasks. They were divided into three age groups: 19 to 32 years, 33 to 43 years, and 44 to 60 years (n = 28 women per group). Baseline systolic blood pressure (SBP) and diastolic blood pressure increased with age, whereas skin conductance level was lower in older women. In addition, initial SBP reactions to tasks were positively related to age, even after controlling for baseline blood pressure, aerobic fitness, and Framingham Type A Scale behavior scores. There were no differences in heart rate (HR) or &quot;additional&quot; HR reactions, so the anticipated decline in cardiac sympathetic response with age was not observed. The mechanisms underlying age-related reactions to mental stress are discussed.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
